{"question_id": "V2-dev-BOOK-en-T1-001", "approach": "SETR_NOFILL", "approach_base": "SETR_NOFILL", "flags": {}, "setting": "en-en", "q_lang": "en", "d_lang": "en", "corpus": "K", "corpus_set": "BOOK_en", "answer_text": "Faraday explains that the candle's heat creates an ascending current of air that cools the outer sides, keeping the edge cooler than the interior, so only the inner part melts while the outside stays solid, forming the cup ([2], [1]). The flame runs down the wick melting the inner fuel until extinguished, but the cooled exterior does not melt ([2]). This regular ascending current playing on all sides keeps the cup horizontal; if disturbed, the fluid runs over in guttering ([1]). The current also supplies the flame with air, as Hooke showed ([3]). Only fuel that forms this cup can serve as a candle, except Irish bogwood, which holds its own fuel like a sponge ([1], [4]). Finally, the melted fuel extinguishes the flame at the sides because it has not been heated enough to burn, unlike fuel carried into the wick in small quantities ([5]).", "abstained": false, "retrieved_units": [{"text": "[stripped: 842 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::o029", "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::p1_b47"], "kind": "chunk", "score": 0.031514, "rank": 1, "span": [13898, 14740], "meta": {"chunk": 2552, "mode": "window", "partial_unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::p1_b47"], "ranks": {"bm25": 2, "dense": 5}, "scores": {"bm25": 13.326733, "dense": 0.681424}, "rrf": 0.031514}}, {"text": "[stripped: 943 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::o029", "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::p1_b46", "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-o